{"cuisine/vietnamese-americans-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/vietnamese-americans-cuisine","n":"Vietnamese-American cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["Vietnamese Americans are Americans of Vietnamese ancestry. They constitute the largest part of the Vietnamese diaspora. As of 2023, over 2.3 million people of Vietnamese descent live in the United States, making them the fourth largest Asian American ethnic group. The majority (about 60%) are immigrants, while 40% were born in the United States."],["The Vietnamese American population grew significantly after 1975, when a large wave of South Vietnamese refugees arrived in the U.S. following the end of the Vietnam War. Rooted in the experience of communist violence and oppression, the Vietnamese American community is marked by a prevalent anticommunist and oftentimes conservative identity. Today, over half of Vietnamese-Americans reside in California and Texas, particularly in metropolitan areas like Los Angeles, Houston, and San Jose."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"vietnamese-americans-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnamese_Americans#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Viet World Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"vietworldkitchen.com","note":"Andrea Nguyen's long-running site on Vietnamese cooking in American kitchens.","url":"https://vietworldkitchen.com/?p=6967","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Vietnamese Food Any Day: Simple Recipes for True, Fresh Flavors","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Andrea Nguyen, 2019","note":"Vietnamese dishes adapted to ingredients from ordinary American grocery stores.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780399580352","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780399580352"},{"name":"The Memory of Taste: Vietnamese American Recipes from Phú Quốc, Oakland, and the Spaces Between","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Tu David Phu","note":"Vietnamese American cooking from an Oakland-born chef with family roots in Phú Quốc.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781984861917","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781984861917"}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Fusion cuisine","cuisine/fusion-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/shanghai-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/shanghai-cuisine","n":"Shanghai cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Shanghai cuisine, also known as Hu cuisine (simplified Chinese: 沪菜; traditional Chinese: 滬菜; pinyin: Hù cài; Shanghainese: wu⁶ tshe¹; IPA: [ɦu¹¹ tsʰᴇ⁴⁴]), is a popular style of Chinese food. In a narrow sense, Shanghai cuisine refers only to what is traditionally called Benbang cuisine (本帮菜; 本幫菜; Běnbāng cài; pen⁵ paon¹ tshe⁵; 'local cuisine') which originated in Shanghai. In a broader sense, it refers to complex styles of cooking developed under the influence of neighboring Jiangsu and Zhejiang provinces."],["The dishes within the cuisine need to master the three elements of \"color, aroma, and taste\". Like other cuisines within China, Shanghai cuisine emphasizes the use of seasonings, the quality of raw ingredients, and preserving the original flavors of ingredients. The adoption of Western influence in Shanghai cuisine resulted in a unique cooking style known as Haipai cuisine (海派菜)."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"shanghai-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanghai_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Land of Fish and Rice: Recipes from the Culinary Heart of China","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Fuchsia Dunlop, 2016","note":"Covers the Jiangnan region, including Shanghai-style cooking.","url":"https://wwnorton.com/books/Land-of-Fish-and-Rice/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Shanghai cooking","count":1,"links":[["Haipai cuisine","cuisine/haipai-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/b/bd/Gekochte_Wollhandkrabben.jpg/500px-Gekochte_Wollhandkrabben.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"J. Patrick Fischer","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gekochte_Wollhandkrabben.jpg"}},"cuisine/manipuri-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/manipuri-cuisine","n":"Manipuri cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["North East Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-east-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Manipuri cuisine refers to the cuisine of Manipur, a state of northeastern India. Daily meals are based on rice, with a few side dishes of vegetables, fish and meat. A meal would usually have a vegetable stew called ensaang or athongba, flavored with dried or fried fish; stir-fried vegetables called kanghou; and a spicy item, which could be morok metpa (a chili paste), eromba (boiled and mashed vegetables with chili and fermented fish), or singju (a piquant salad). All piquant side dishes are accompanied by a choice of fresh herbs, collectively called maroi. The base and essence of both Meitei Manipuri and non-Meitei (such as Naga and Kuki) Manipuri cuisine is the fermented fish called ngari. Several dishes of meat, mostly chicken and pork, are cooked with unique recipes. Due to religious taboos, Muslims living in Manipur do not cook pork."],["A side dish of steamed (a-ngaanba) or boiled vegetables with a hint of sugar (cham-phut) are also quite common as palate cleansers in most meals. The aromatics of most dishes start with frying bay leaf, chives, onion, garlic, and ginger in mustard oil. The rest of the vegetables follow after that. Oil is sparingly used in most of the main stews but the side dishes of kanghou (stir-fried spicy vegetables) and bora (fritters) make up for that. Fish is also a staple, and appears in every meal, either as ngaari or as roasted or fried pieces. While fish is an essential part of the diet, due to increasing prices, fish curry is prepared only occasionally, or during feasts. The Meiteis live in the valley of Manipur where freshwater fish from lakes and rivers and ponds had been plentiful until recent times. In Manipur, meals are known as chakluk."],["Non-Meitei cuisine can be found in the hills of the state."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"manipuri-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manipuri_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Chakluk","dish/chakluk"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Lukthel","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lukthel.com","note":"Recipe site dedicated to Manipuri dishes, organized by community of origin.","url":"https://lukthel.com/blog/15/exploring-the-culinary-delights-of-manipuri-cuisine","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["North East Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-east-indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Within Manipuri cooking","count":1,"links":[["Meitei cuisine","cuisine/meitei-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/d/d5/Manipur_food_includes_Bamboo_shoot_veg%2C_Lotus_veg_etc.JPG/500px-Manipur_food_includes_Bamboo_shoot_veg%2C_Lotus_veg_etc.JPG","w":500,"h":456,"by":"Prasadsovani","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manipur_food_includes_Bamboo_shoot_veg%2C_Lotus_veg_etc.JPG","of":"Chakluk"}},"cuisine/cappadocian-greeks-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cappadocian-greeks-cuisine","n":"Cappadocian Greek cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Greece","intro":[["The Cappadocian Greeks, or simply Cappadocians, are an ethnic Greek community native to the geographical region of Cappadocia in central-eastern Anatolia; roughly the Nevşehir, Kayseri, Konya provinces and their surroundings in modern-day Turkey. There had been a continuous Greek presence in Cappadocia since antiquity, and by at least the 5th century AD the Greek language had become the lingua franca of the region."],["In the late 11th century, Seljuq Turks arriving from Central Asia conquered the region after the battle of Mantzikert, beginning its gradual shift in language and religion. In 1923, following the mass killing of Christian Ottomans across Anatolia, the surviving Cappadocian communities were forced to leave their native homeland and resettle in Greece by the terms of the Greek–Turkish population exchange. Today their descendants can be found throughout Greece and the Greek diaspora worldwide."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cappadocian-greeks-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cappadocian_Greeks#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Greek cuisine","cuisine/greek-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/d/db/Cappadocian_Greeks.JPG/500px-Cappadocian_Greeks.JPG","w":500,"h":312,"by":"Zorlusert","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cappadocian_Greeks.JPG"}},"cuisine/roman-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/roman-cuisine","n":"Lazian or Roman","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["Roman cuisine is the cooking traditions and practices of the Italian city of Rome. It features fresh, seasonal and simply-prepared ingredients from the Roman Campagna. These include peas, globe artichokes and fava beans, shellfish, milk-fed lamb and goat, and cheeses such as pecorino romano and ricotta. Olive oil is used mostly to dress raw vegetables, while strutto (pork lard) and fat from prosciutto are preferred for frying. The most popular sweets in Rome are small individual pastries called pasticcini, gelato and handmade chocolates and candies. Special dishes are often reserved for different days of the week; for example, gnocchi is eaten on Thursdays, baccalà (salted cod) on Fridays and trippa (offal) on Saturdays."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"roman-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Abbacchio","dish/abbacchio"],["Cacio e pepe","dish/cacio-e-pepe"],["Coda alla vaccinara","dish/coda-alla-vaccinara"],["Pinsa (bread)","dish/pinsa-bread"],["Roman pizza","dish/roman-pizza"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Tasting Rome","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Katie Parla, Kristina Gill, 2016","note":"Classic and contemporary Roman dishes from two Rome-based food writers.","url":"","suggested":true},{"name":"Cooking the Roman Way","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"David Downie","note":"Recipes from Roman home cooks and trattorias, with background on the city's food.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780062031099","suggested":true,"isbn":"9780062031099"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Pasta","ingredient/pasta"],["Pecorino Romano PDO","ingredient/pecorino-romano-pdo"],["Pecorino romano","ingredient/pecorino-romano"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Spaghetti","ingredient/spaghetti"],["Spaghetti alla chitarra","ingredient/spaghetti-alla-chitarra"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Oxtail","ingredient/oxtail"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/world-cuisine/img/thumb/a/a3/Spaghetto_alla_chitarra_alla_carbonara.jpg/500px-Spaghetto_alla_chitarra_alla_carbonara.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Sergio D’Afflitto","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spaghetto_alla_chitarra_alla_carbonara.jpg"}},"dish/bara-brith":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/bara-brith","n":"Bara brith","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Bara brith is a traditional Welsh tea bread flavoured with tea, dried fruits and spices. It is similar to fruitcake."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-bara-brith"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bara_brith"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Argentine cuisine","cuisine/argentine-cuisine"],["Welsh cuisine","cuisine/welsh-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Candied peel","ingredient/candied-peel"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Zante currant","ingredient/zante-currant"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Familia Kitchen: Argentina","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"familiakitchen.com","note":"Argentine recipes and stories on a Latino family cooking site.","url":"https://familiakitchen.com/destination/argentina/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Baking with Granny: Welsh recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"bakingwithgranny.co.uk","note":"Traditional Welsh baking recipes such as Welsh cakes and bara brith.","url":"https://bakingwithgranny.co.uk/category/recipe/welsh/","suggested":true,"kind":"blo